<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.4.2: Exploring Linear, Exponential, and Quadratic Growth</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01795</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.4.2: Exploring Linear, Exponential, and Quadratic Growth</md:title>
    <md:uuid>81cb7af7-3a5f-4cde-ae9b-2512c989d585</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>In this activity, you will compare two different patterns. In each pattern, the number of small squares is a function
  of the step number, \(n\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> In Pattern A, the length and width of the rectangle grow by 1 small square from each step to the next.</item>
</list>

<para>Pattern A</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Four steps of a growing pattern. Step 0 is blank. Step 1: One square. Step 2: Four squares in a two by two square. Step 3: Nine squares in a three by three square."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/0e7b14cef050d298b7cc5ac185823ad0e4ef3fc4"/></media></figure></para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Write an equation to represent the number of small squares at Step \(n\) in Pattern A. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    \(f(n)=n^2\)

  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="9075cc7a-5970-44d6-85ea-a95a6b92fcbc" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q#-->
  <exercise data-content-id="b03ffaee-7ef7-4c98-8cf3-161785ba20bf" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-solution="quadratic" data-solution-options="[&quot;linear&quot;, &quot;quadratic&quot;, &quot;exponential&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Is the function in Pattern A linear, quadratic, or exponential? </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! It is quadratic. </para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
